   "Paging splits memory into fixed-size frames and a process into pages of the same size, removing external fragmentation. Virtual memory lets a process use more memory than physical RAM by keeping some pages on disk; a page fault occurs when a required page is not in main memory and must be loaded. Page replacement algorithms such as FIFO and LRU choose which page to remove.",
